 It excludes every runtime descriptor and is scanned for private-key and
 password markers before output.
 
+Before provisioning begins, each built-in provider writes a task-private
+`preparing` ownership journal. Every spawned endpoint or sshd PID is appended
+atomically before the next provisioning boundary, and Lima ownership is marked
+before its start command. Teardown reconciles that journal with processes still
+owned by the current provider process. A partial macOS, Linux, or real-host
+prepare therefore remains discoverable by a later teardown invocation; failure
+to terminate any row prevents zero-residual evidence, while teardown continues
+attempting every previously prepared row.
+
 Linux teardown enumerates the task-named Lima instance, fails closed on list or
 delete errors and timeouts, and enumerates again after deletion. The provider
 retains its task state for a safe cleanup retry until absence is positively
